  "text": "Dorcas sewed so much love that the widows brought the evidence. Acts nine. In Joppa there is a disciple named Tabitha, which by interpretation is Dorcas, and the text says she was full of good works and almsdeeds which she did. Then she gets sick and she dies. They wash her and lay her in an upper chamber. Lydda is near Joppa, and the disciples hear Peter is there, so they send two men asking him to come without delay. And when Peter arrives, they bring him upstairs into that room. And here is the scene. All the widows stood by him weeping, and shewing the coats and garments which Dorcas made, while she was with them. They are holding up the clothes. That is the eulogy. Not a speech. Evidence. Widows in that world had almost no safety net, and she had been making them clothes with her own hands. Peter puts them all out of the room, kneels down, prays, turns to the body and says, Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes, and sat up. And he called the saints and widows and presented her alive.",
